---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **이웃 차트가 신호인가.** (`/lead-lag`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **물음이 성립하는지부터 낸다.** 절반 넘게 같은 주에 뜬다 — 「먼저」랄 것이 없다.
 * ⛔ **죽은 가설을 그대로 싣는다.** 이웃 신호는 없다. 있는 것은 **하나의 줄**이다.
 * ⛔ **나라를 줄세워 내지 않는다.** 줄이 있다는 것만 내고 순서는 안 낸다.
 *    ⚠ 순서를 내면 「빠른 나라 순위표」가 되고, 그건 우리가 안 하기로 한 것이다.
 * ⛔ 왜 그런지는 이 자료에 **없다.**
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-lead-lag.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-lead-lag.json';

const 나라수 = new Set(data.pairs.flatMap((x) => x.pair.split('–'))).size;
const 고리 = data.triangles - data.transitiveTriangles;

const TITLE = 'No country\'s Netflix chart is a signal for another\'s — they are one queue';
const DESC =
  `Of ${data.observations.toLocaleString('en-US')} times a Korean series reached two countries' charts, `
  + `${data.sameWeekPc}% arrived in both the same week. Where one did come first, the direction almost never `
  + `reverses: ${data.transitivePc}% of ${data.triangles.toLocaleString('en-US')} country triples are consistent, `
  + `so the markets form a single order rather than a web of signals.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/lead-lag">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A scheduler with a neighbouring market on screen wants to know whether it tells them anything.
    If Thailand takes a Korean series this week, is Vietnam next? This page answers that, and the
    answer is not the one the question expects.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>First: most of the time there is no "first"</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>Why this comes before anything else.</b> {data.whySameWeekFirst}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>A Korean series that reached both countries' charts</th><th>Times</th><th>Share</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Arrived in both the same week</td>
            <td>{data.sameWeek.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.sameWeekPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">One came first</td>
            <td>{(data.observations - data.sameWeek).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{(100 - data.sameWeekPc).toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{data.sameWeekPc}% of the time nobody leads.</b> Netflix puts a series into every country at
      once, and more often than not it lands on both charts in the same week. Any leading-indicator
      story is confined from the start to the other {(100 - data.sameWeekPc).toFixed(1)}%.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where one does come first, the direction hardly ever flips</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">
      For each pair of countries we counted how lopsided the ordering is: 0 means the two swap the
      lead evenly, 50 means one of them is always first. {data.pairsMeasured} pairs had at least
      {' '}{data.minimumTitlesPerPair} titles arriving in different weeks.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>How lopsided the pair is</th><th>Pairs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.skewBands.map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.pairs === Math.max(...data.skewBands.map((x) => x.pairs)) ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td class="tot">{b.pairs}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The median pair sits <b>{data.medianSkew} points from even</b>, out of a possible 50. When one
      of two countries takes a Korean series first, it is usually the same one of the two — every
      time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>But that is not a signal. It is a queue.</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.whyTransitivity}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Countries with enough data to place</td><td class="tot">{나라수}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Country triples checked</td><td class="tot">{data.triangles.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Triples that are consistent</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.transitiveTriangles.toLocaleString('en-US')} — {data.transitivePc}%</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Triples that form a loop</td><td class="tot">{고리}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{고리} loops out of {data.triangles.toLocaleString('en-US')}.</b> If A takes a title before B
      and B before C, then A takes it before C — essentially always. That is what it looks like when
      {' '}{나라수} markets are standing in <b>one queue</b>, not passing signals to each other.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>So the answer to the question is no.</b> Watching a neighbour tells you nothing that
      knowing your own position does not already tell you, and your own position does not change
      from title to title. A scheduler needs one fact — am I early or late — and after that the
      neighbour's chart is redundant.
    </p>
    <p class="note fine">
      ⚠ We are not publishing the order itself. Ranking {나라수} markets by how fast they take Korean
      titles would be a league table, and a league table is the one thing this publication does not
      make. The finding is that an order exists and is stable; which end a given market sits at is a
      question for that market's own sheet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How much this rests on</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart rows read</td><td class="tot">{data.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Korean series rows</td><td class="tot">{data.koreanRows.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Weeks</td><td class="tot">{data.weeksSpanned}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Korean series that charted in two or more markets</td><td class="tot">{data.titlesInTwoOrMoreMarkets}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Country pairs seen at all</td><td class="tot">{data.countryPairsObserved.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Country pairs with enough titles to measure</td><td class="tot">{data.pairsMeasured}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{data.countryPairsObserved.toLocaleString('en-US')} pairs were seen and only {data.pairsMeasured}
      could be measured.</b> A pair needs at least {data.minimumTitlesPerPair} titles that arrived in
      different weeks, and most pairs of countries have never shared that many Korean series with a
      gap between them. The {data.minimumTitlesPerPair} is our threshold, not Netflix's.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/lead-lag" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why this cannot say why.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Arrival is a chart entry, not a release.</b> Netflix does not publish release dates by
        country, so "first" here means first to appear in a top 10 — which a small market can reach
        weeks after a title is available in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/arrival">How titles arrive and spread</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/foothold">Does an earlier hit help the next one</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
